ld move to cut off  supplies completely countries are now  trying to refill storage sites before  winter and the heating season mike  armstrong global news  the police's point of view  ahead the body cam video from the uvalde  school mass shooting  a leading figure of the freedom convoy  movement has been released on bail  [Music]  pat king spent five months in an ottawa  jail for his role in leading the  protests that choked the city's downtown  core for weeks  king is facing a raft of criminal  charges in connection to those protests  including mischief intimidation and  obstructing police and in april he was  also charged with perjury and  obstruction of justice there is a  publication ban on the evidence and  reasons for the court's decision but  king will need to abide by multiple  conditions as part of his release  a new report into the uvalde school  shooting describes what's being called a  systematic breakdown highlighting how  the safeguards designed to protect  students failed  and newly released police body cam  footage shows the chaos of that horrific  day when 19 children and two teachers  were killed jackson prescott reports and  a warning some of the images in this  story are disturbing  almost immediately police came under  fire as they rushed into robb  elementary the moments that followed  descended into a chaotic uncoordinated  response we got to get in there  we got to get in there and just keep  shooting  we got to get in there police spent more  than an hour waiting for instructions as  nearly 400 officers descended on the  school  as bursts of gunfire erupted from a  classroom school district police chief  pete arendando even tried to negotiate  with the suspect  this could be peaceful  outside other officers rushed to break  windows and rescue students  no one breached the classroom until it  was too late if you know there is active  shooting active killing going on or  somebody is dying  the standard is  you have to continue to do something to  stop that killing or stop that dialing  the first report into the massacre found  officers put their own safety over that  of the innocent victims there was no  clear leadership no proper communication  they don't protect nothing their powers  the school itself had issues too poor  wi-fi delayed lockdown alerts no one  used the school intercom to communicate  the threat the police failed us the  school system failed us those failures  costed our son his life the report  concludes most of the victims died  within minutes but some could have been  saved if police had acted sooner  this devastating look at police actions  has left families wondering if anyone  will be held accountable for the  failures of that day  prosko global news washington  in florida the trial for the parkland  school shooter started today  family members of the 17 people shot and  killed at marjory stoneman douglas high  school in 2018 were at the courthouse  for opening statements  in october nicholas cruz who is now 23  years old pleaded guilty to 17 counts of  first degree murder  jurors will decide whether he is  sentenced to death or gets life in  prison without parole  and jury selection has started at the  trial of donald trump's former chief  strategist steve bannon is facing  charges of criminal contempt of congress  after initially defying a subpoena  issued by the committee investigating  the january 6th attack on the u.s  capitol  the trial is going ahead even though  earlier this month bannon notified the  committee he is now willing to testify  if he's found guilty he faces up to two  years behind bars  atoning for abuse next how the catholic  church plans to compensate victims  ahead of pope francis's visit to canada  next week the supreme court of  newfoundland and labrador has approved  the sale of 42 properties belonging to  the catholic church including 12  churches the money from the sale will be  used to settle legal claims by victims  of sexual and physical abuse at a  church-run orphanage as ross lord  reports the sale items contain rich  history and even papal connections  even decades after the systemic abuse of  boys at newfoundland's mount castle  orphanage accountability is slow at  nighttime they come to your bed i know i  won't describe it in detail compensating  more than 100 victims most of whom are  now elderly men will cost the catholic  church an estimated tens of millions of  dollars to raise the money church  leaders in saint john's are turning to  the real estate market in an update to  parishioners archbishop peter hunt said  42 properties are for sale including 12  churches and that only a few will  continue to be used as catholic churches  the archbishop called it an emotional  and distressing process the properties  being sold are worth an estimated 20  million dollars among them this grotto  in the community of flat rock where pope  john paul ii visited in 1984 kneeling to  pray and bestowing a special blessing on  the grotto but the big stunner is this  the province's most famous church the  basilica of saint john the baptist the  basilica has stood as a stately landmark  in saint john's since it was built  almost two centuries ago court documents  show the basilica will be sold to a  heritage foundation which promises to  preserve it what has happened here  seems to have been the best possible  resolution in that the basilica will  still be available  for the catholic community but the value  of the basilica will go towards  compensating survivors the catholic  church has had trouble following through  on promises church entities agreed to  raise 25 million dollars for residential  school survivors in canada then came up  with less than 4 million dollars  officials suggest dozens of other  newfoundland church properties could go  on the block promising they intend to  meet their legal obligations to victims  while restructuring their organization  in a positive way  ross lord global news  and that is global national for this  monday night i'm tracy tong tonight's  your canada is lower island cove  newfoundland and labrador thanks for  watching jeff semple will be at the  anchor desk tomorrow have a good night  [Music]
 

